(function executeRule(current, previous) {
    if (current.active == false) {
        current.state = 7;
    }
})(current, previous);

questions.modelAnswer:
A Business Rule on the base task table with no table-specific scoping runs for every table that extends it — Incident, Problem, and Change alike. The script sets state = 7, which is Closed for Incident, but Problem and Change have entirely different state models, so this rule silently corrupts their state field whenever active is set to false on those tables too. The fix is checking current.sys_class_name before applying table-specific logic, so the rule only behaves this way for Incident and leaves other extending tables untouched:

(function executeRule(current, previous) {
    if (current.active == false && current.sys_class_name == 'incident') {
        current.state = 7;
    }
})(current, previous);

The better long-term fix is adding a Filter Condition restricting the rule to the incident table specifically (or moving the rule onto the Incident table extension itself rather than task), so the restriction is visible in the Business Rule record's configuration, not buried only inside the script logic where a future reviewer might miss it.

The server-side piece is an AbstractAjaxProcessor-extending Script Include, since that's the required base class for anything called via GlideAjax, reading parameters with getParameter() rather than typed arguments:

var ApprovalAjax = Class.create();
ApprovalAjax.prototype = Object.extendsObject(AbstractAjaxProcessor, {
    isAuthorizedApprover: function() {
        var userId = this.getParameter('sysparm_user');
        var groupId = this.getParameter('sysparm_group');
        var gr = new GlideRecord('sys_user_grmember');
        gr.addQuery('user', userId);
        gr.addQuery('group', groupId);
        gr.setLimit(1);
        gr.query();
        return gr.next() ? 'true' : 'false';
    },
    type: 'ApprovalAjax'
});

The client script calls it asynchronously, never assuming a synchronous return:

function onSubmit() {
    var ga = new GlideAjax('ApprovalAjax');
    ga.addParam('sysparm_name', 'isAuthorizedApprover');
    ga.addParam('sysparm_user', g_form.getValue('requested_for'));
    ga.addParam('sysparm_group', g_form.getValue('assignment_group'));
    ga.getXMLAnswer(function(answer) {
        if (answer !== 'true') {
            g_form.addErrorMessage('Selected user is not an authorized approver for this group.');
            g_form.submit = false;
        }
    });
    return false;
}

The key points: the Script Include never trusts the client, re-validates the authorization server-side rather than trusting a value the browser sends; GlideAjax parameters are always strings; and getXMLAnswer's callback is asynchronous, so onSubmit must return false and let the callback control actual submission rather than returning a synchronous true/false.

The correct approach is marking the specific Script Include method Team B's application exposes as 'Accessible from: All application scopes' (or a scoped access level if Application Access rules are used), rather than opening the entire application or its tables to cross-scope access. On the Script Include record itself, the Client Callable and Accessible From fields control this precisely at the API level:

var SharedNotificationUtils = Class.create();
SharedNotificationUtils.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {},
    sendApprovalReminder: function(approvalSysId) {
        // reusable logic Team A's app can now call
    },
    type: 'SharedNotificationUtils'
};
// Script Include record: Accessible from = "All application scopes"

Team A then calls it exactly as it would any other API:

new x_teamb_app.SharedNotificationUtils().sendApprovalReminder(approvalId);

Granting broader access — making Team B's tables or every Script Include in the app cross-scope accessible — would be the wrong call because it exposes far more surface area than the one method Team A actually needs, recreating tight coupling between apps that were supposed to stay independent, and making it impossible to later refactor Team B's internals without risking a break in Team A's app that nobody flagged as a dependency. The minimal, specific grant keeps the dependency visible and narrow.

ServiceNow Scripted REST APIs support explicit version records under a single API, each with its own resource implementation while sharing the same base path and API ID. The correct approach is creating a new version (e.g. v2) of the resource rather than modifying the existing v1 resource's response shape in place, so the three existing consumers keep calling /api/x_app/incidents (defaulting to v1, or explicitly /v1/) unaffected, while the new consumer calls /api/x_app/incidents with an Accept-Version header or explicit /v2/ path requesting the new shape:

// v1 resource script — unchanged, still returns the original shape
(function process(request, response) {
    response.setBody({ incident_number: current.number, desc: current.short_description });
})(request, response);

// v2 resource script — new shape for the new consumer
(function process(request, response) {
    response.setBody({
        number: current.number,
        description: current.short_description,
        priority: current.priority.getDisplayValue()
    });
})(request, response);

The existing three consumers require zero code changes since v1 keeps its contract exactly as before; the new consumer is pointed at v2 explicitly. Mutating v1's response shape in place instead would break all three existing integrations silently, with no way for them to opt in or out of the change.

Basic Auth sends the username and password on every request, in a credential that doesn't expire on its own and is rarely rotated once set — if intercepted or leaked, it grants access indefinitely until someone manually changes it. Migrating to OAuth 2.0 means: first, registering an OAuth Entity Profile / Application Registry record on the ServiceNow side with the client ID and client secret the vendor issued; second, configuring the REST Message's connection to use 'OAuth 2.0' as the authentication type and pointing it at that OAuth profile instead of the Basic Auth credential record; third, confirming the OAuth grant type matches what the vendor supports — client credentials grant is typical for server-to-server integrations with no end-user context.

var request = new sn_ws.RESTMessageV2('VendorAPI', 'getRecords');
// Authentication is now resolved via the REST Message's configured OAuth profile,
// not set inline — the profile handles token acquisition and refresh automatically
var response = request.execute();

ServiceNow automatically requests and caches the access token using the registered OAuth profile, and refreshes it before expiry, so the script itself doesn't manage tokens manually. The real win isn't just 'more modern' — tokens are short-lived and scoped, so a leaked token expires and grants narrower access than a leaked permanent password would.

Two separate things have to be right: the retry loop must wait progressively longer between attempts (exponential backoff), and the operation being retried must be idempotent so a successful-but-lost response doesn't create a duplicate on retry.

function upsertAlertRecord(externalId, alertData) {
    var gr = new GlideRecord('incident');
    gr.addQuery('u_external_alert_id', externalId); // coalesce field
    gr.query();
    if (gr.next()) {
        gr.short_description = alertData.description;
        gr.update();
    } else {
        gr.initialize();
        gr.u_external_alert_id = externalId;
        gr.short_description = alertData.description;
        gr.insert();
    }
}

function callWithBackoff(externalId, alertData, maxRetries) {
    var backoffMs = 1000;
    var attempt = 0;
    while (attempt <= maxRetries) {
        var request = new sn_ws.RESTMessageV2('AlertWebhook', 'postAlert');
        request.setRequestBody(JSON.stringify(alertData));
        var response = request.execute();
        if (response.getStatusCode() == 200) {
            upsertAlertRecord(externalId, alertData);
            return true;
        }
        attempt++;
        if (attempt <= maxRetries) {
            gs.sleep(backoffMs);
            backoffMs = backoffMs * 2;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

u_external_alert_id is the coalesce field: whether upsertAlertRecord runs once or three times because the first two responses timed out, the end state is exactly one incident record, which is what makes it safe to wrap in the retry loop at all.

The fix is wrapping the resource logic in a try/catch that logs enough context to diagnose a failure after the fact, and returning a structured error response instead of letting an unhandled exception produce a generic, undiagnosable 500.

(function process(request, response) {
    try {
        var body = request.body.data;
        var gr = new GlideRecord('incident');
        gr.addQuery('number', body.incident_number);
        gr.query();
        if (!gr.next()) {
            response.setStatus(404);
            response.setBody({ error: 'Incident not found', incident_number: body.incident_number });
            return;
        }
        gr.setValue('work_notes', body.note);
        gr.update();
        response.setStatus(200);
        response.setBody({ result: 'updated', incident_number: body.incident_number });
    } catch (ex) {
        gs.error('VendorIncidentAPI error: ' + ex.message + ' | payload: ' + JSON.stringify(request.body.data));
        response.setStatus(500);
        response.setBody({ error: 'Internal error processing request' });
    }
})(request, response);

The gs.error() call logs the exact exception message and the incoming payload to the system log, giving a precise, searchable record of what request triggered the failure, rather than depending on the vendor's own incomplete error report. The 404 branch also distinguishes a genuinely bad request (incident not found) from a real server-side failure, instead of letting both collapse into the same generic unhandled 500.
